The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-4x^3+5x}{2x^3+7}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-4x^3+5x}{x^{3}}}{\dfrac{2x^3+7}{x^{3}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-4\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}+\dfrac{5\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x^2}}{\dfrac{2\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}+\dfrac{7}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-4+\dfrac{5}{x^2}}{2+\dfrac{7}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-4+0}{2+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-2
\end{align}$
